docs/harnessloop-review-20260716.findings.json:28:   "verdict_reason": "逐条复核全部属实：(1) plugins/harnessloop/skills/harnessloop-loop/SKILL.md:57 Core Contract 为 'real data, dynamic data, repository source, or source-data files'（无 runtime），:436-443 Verification Phase 清单同样只有 static/dynamic/source/source-data 四项；(2) 同文件 :3 frontmatter 写 'static/dynamic/runtime/source'，:328-341 Round Structure 含 evidence/runtime/ 目录，自相矛盾；(3) references/evidence-index-template.md:32-38、references/eval-matrix-template.md:11、harnessloop-evidence/SKILL.md:19 均为含 human-confirmation 的 5 类；(4) README.md:158-163 只列 4 类；(5) docs/usage.md 与 docs/harnessloop-framework.md 的验证清单均含 runtime；(6) 全仓 grep 确认 human-confirmation 证据在 rounds/NNNN/evidence/{static,dynamic,runtime,source}/ 及所有模板（decision/control-contract/adversarial-review）中均无落盘位置说明；(7) verify_protocol.py 只做 scope-lock 包含与引用存在性检查，validate.py 文档一致性阶段只对 init_project.py 骨架断言，枚举无机械校验，建议可行。severity 由 high 降为 medium：同一 SKILL 文件内 frontmatter、round 结构、自审信号（:315 'Add missing runtime validation'、:447 'missing runtime coverage'）多处提及 runtime，agent 完全遗漏 runtime 验证的实际风险被缓解；human-confirmation 落盘缺口影响小；无安全/数据后果，属真实但中等的协议枚举漂移。",
docs/harnessloop-review-20260716.findings.json:938:   "verdict_reason": "Every factual claim verified against source: status enum (SKILL.md:37) is exactly 'initialized | not-initialized | inconsistent | blocked' with no setup-incomplete; continue enum (SKILL.md:57) has no needs-setup; verify_protocol.py enforces only Rules A/B; loop SKILL.md:291 names self-check.md as the 'setup and continuation gate checks' record yet no skill defines the setup gate's trigger, owner, or pass criteria (only delegation fields are specified at loop:413); init_project.py copies exactly 12 files verbatim from templates that are empty colon-lines/empty tables, making the suggested byte-compare/empty-field Rule C genuinely machine-checkable. No other file covers the gap: gap-review is intake-only, goal's missing-fields action is goal-only. The suggestion violates no hard principle — the check is read-only and deterministic, status stays read-only, needs-setup asks rather than infers or writes. Severity downgraded to medium because soft partial coverage exists (continue's contract-insufficient blocker, loop's 'ask the user to fill in data-source connection requirements' and 'create the smallest setup...request instead of inventing facts', init's next-step pointer), so a compliant model should still ask the user — the gap is that the machine-checkable surfaces cannot express it, which is a guidance/onboarding deficiency rather than a safety or wrong-execution defect.",
docs/harnessloop-review-20260716.findings.json:1073:   "verdict_reason": "Gap is real: loop SKILL.md:114 quote verified verbatim; data-sources-template.md:8-31 is 5 header-only tables; usage.md:61 verified; zero scan keywords in both SKILL.md files and no scanning logic in the 3 bundled scripts (init_project.py/round_cost.py/verify_protocol.py); init SKILL.md:23 even says \"Do not infer project facts... data sources.\" Across all 12 skills and 27 templates the only related coverage is goal-breakdown-template.md's Read-Only Discovery Plan (\"Runtime validation options\") which runs after setup and never feeds back into setup/data-sources.md — so users do face 5 blank tables at setup. The mock example itself records `npm test -- --runInBand`, proving such facts are repo-detectable. The suggestion does not violate hard principles: scan-with-citation + Status=proposed-unconfirmed + grouped user confirmation is detection, not invention, and matches the framework's own evidence ethos (repository source is a declared evidence class, every claim must cite paths); no tool substitution; the no-unconfirmed-write rules are scoped to external systems while .harnessloop files are protocol-owned. Only mild tension is init's \"do not infer project facts\" wording, resolvable by placing the scan as an explicit confirmed setup step as the suggestion proposes. Severity downgraded: one-time onboarding friction, not correctness/safety; partial downstream mitigation via discovery handoffs and the filled mock example — medium, not high.",
docs/harnessloop-review-20260716.findings.json:1148:   "verdict_reason": "Core gap verified: init_project.py main() (lines 147-155) prints only the project path and created_dirs/written/skipped file lists with no next-step guidance, while usage.md:36-39 and README.md:92-96 present the script as a first-class init path; the \"$harnessloop-loop next\" pointer as an output requirement exists only in harnessloop-init SKILL.md line 90 (\"Next recommended prompt...\"), which the script path never triggers. The suggestion is feasible and violates no hard principles — a static stdout epilogue fabricates no project facts (file list comes from real results; skip/--force text matches write_file behavior at lines 68-71), performs no writes, substitutes no tools, and is low-noise. However the finding overstates: \"script-path users get nothing / guidance only lives in SKILL.md\" is inaccurate — README.md line 124 (\"Then use $harnessloop-loop to define a goal...\") sits directly under the script command, and usage.md lines 47-61 describe every created file's purpose, so any user who discovered the script via docs has next steps one paragraph away. The real gap is narrower (script stdout not self-sufficient, affecting terminal-only reruns and agent relay outside harnessloop-init). One suggestion nit: the proposed fill order (environment → control-contract → data-sources → cost-context-policy) is not defined anywhere in the protocol and would be new guidance, not restored coverage. Severity low is correctly calibrated — this is a polish item, not a functional defect.",
docs/harnessloop-review-20260716.findings.json:1253:   "verdict_reason": "Core gap verified by actually running the script: main() (init_project.py L147-155) prints only project path + created_dirs/written lists + intake_path, with no next-step guidance; the 'Next recommended prompt' exists solely in harnessloop-init/SKILL.md L90 (agent-mediated), and grep across all 12 SKILL.md files plus scripts confirms no other stdout-facing coverage. docs/usage.md L36-39 and README.md L95 do present direct script invocation as a first-class route. The suggestion violates no hard principle (static print, no fabrication, no tool substitution, no writes) and matches existing protocol wording; effort S is accurate. Severity corrected medium→low for three reasons: (1) both documented entry points place the next steps one paragraph from the command (README L124 'Then use $harnessloop-loop...', usage.md L47-61 file purposes, README L128-144 / usage.md L67-84 full intake flow), so users on this path are not truly stranded — only stdout self-sufficiency is missing; (2) the --intake sub-claim is partially wrong: the script already prints intake_path and the written transfer-packet.md path, so the suggestion's 'output the transfer packet target path' is redundant — only the 'run $harnessloop-intake first' reminder is genuinely absent; (3) the primary consumption path (agent skill) already delivers this guidance.",
.hopper/handoffs/T-069-output.md:21:核心 alias-only 命题从 scope-lock、coverage、多轮状态、`--show-root-paths`、`verify_identity=False` 等新面回打后仍成立。不过，T-068 提到的无声明 `@@foo/` detail 差异是规格“逐字节不变”的实质违反；另发现 versioned 声明 symlink、alias 默认不可见、schema 类型等规格落地缺口。结论为 **REWORK**。
